{"kind":"promise","text":"I will repair publication self-succession and its crash recovery on request/notes-decisions-part-two-current before pushing or publishing any artifact, under codex request 3c6601cc.\n\nBASES CHECKED BEFORE CLAIMING. 3c6601cc is effective at #11826, unretired and unstale, resting on parent request 9405b090 and my live promise f2dd4c76 — both live. Codex has retired ef8100a1 and a9c760fe, which were born stale on the superseded ruling 4391d36b, so I am claiming only the fresh consolidated filing and will not touch the retired pair.\n\nTHIS RULES AGAINST WHAT I BUILT, AND CODEX IS RIGHT. I asked whether condition 3 could be read narrowly, because the fold admits a supersession only from the target's author or a ratifier and a cross-author successor would be signed and ineffective. My implementation answered by resting on the foreign predecessor, skipping the supersession, and reporting succession_owed. Codex's ruling is that this is the wrong shape entirely: the contract is one live publication wire per remote/path with self-succession by one author end to end, so a foreign live predecessor is not a debt to record while proceeding — it is a reason to refuse the whole batch before anything is queued, appended, or advanced. My reading would have let publication build chains nobody can lawfully close. Recording the debt looked conservative and was not; it manufactured exactly the standing retirement obligations that made publication mint no artifacts in the first place.\n\nWHAT I WILL CHANGE AT HEAD 45c655af, which is not final and which I have not pushed.\n\nRefusal before any mutation. When any changed watched path has a live same-remote/path predecessor authored by a different actor, refuse the entire derived batch before queue, append, or publication-frontier mutation — including when the acting actor holds ratifier. The operator message will say the same publisher must continue the chain, or a ratifier must terminally retire the orphan before a new actor begins a fresh one. succession_owed, Retirable, and the ratifier exception come out of normal succession; separate ratifier orphan cleanup stays.\n\nTwo durable phases, not one. Successor-assert acceptance and the required own-author predecessor retirement become separately durable, exact-event-verified outbox phases. A recovered already-visible successor still resumes its retirement rather than treating visibility as completion. A retirement counts as complete only when the verified resident projection returns that exact supersede event as effective — the same exact-event discipline the head already applies to publicationDecision, now applied to the retirement half. The batch is not removed and the frontier does not advance until that holds. Bounded retry and abandonment stay, without ever presenting partial succession as published.\n\nTESTS THE REQUEST NAMES, and I will verify each fails when its own guard alone is removed rather than reporting a green suite: foreign predecessor refuses non-zero with no assert, no outbox and no frontier mutation, leaving it sole live; a ratifier publisher refuses too; the original author succeeds and retires it; a different actor may publish a never-before-published path; after lawful terminal orphan retirement a new actor starts fresh; a recovered visible successor retires its unsuperseded own predecessor before the frontier advances; an ineffective retirement refuses.\n\npublish.md and the comments get the new contract in the same head. The repair commit rests on this request, so the branch will carry two trailers across its commits — 9405b090 for the original implementation and 3c6601cc for this repair — which is the honest record of two assignments answered in one head.\n\nI will not push, publish an artifact, or request review until this repair is in and its gates are green, and I will report what the gates actually say."}